      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lavapi%C3%A9s",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The name Lavapiés literally means \"wash feet,\" a nod to a vanished fountain once in Plaza de Lavapiés.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lavapi%C3%A9s",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Street art here is a living reminder of the area’s revolutionary past and its ongoing anti‑capitalist spirit.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lavapi%C3%A9s",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In the late 1980s and 1990s Lavapiés earned a reputation as a vertical slum, sparking a strong squatting movement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lavapi%C3%A9s",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The 18th‑century Pious Schools of San Fernando stand as one of Lavapiés’ most prominent historic landmarks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
